      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Agents",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "fontFamily": 7,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Agents",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.15,
      "created": 1791802801645,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
    {
      "id": "zHi3GDdqOXqSuix_mNGwa",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -7885.287328162547,
      "y": 4260.412648391093,
      "width": 1026.778076171875,
      "height": 700,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "rc6dTnznxxDzl6gxNtt7Q",
        "Z56BMqmuuQjJEHl0j2I2H",
        "ihwi30Raw3HqDRtZDFi3n",
        "FpZcQ5BGxpAXplqIhA_R7",
        "pWgS7Yll-GPEgnTJKSuAb",
        "TtLWZgMTbjse3A07JebUm",
        "eFka5AOZeuvF8MXoOOwhU",
        "o5cdv6d1smtLvqLHRgS_F",
        "dshEba01l96glpOzxnKWt"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b7R",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1151786943,
      "version": 8942,
      "versionNonce": 1092740849,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1792409321299,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "An LLM agent runs tools in a loop to achieve a goal.\n\nAgents begin their work with either a command from, or interactive discussion with, the human user. Once\nthe goal is set, agents plan and interact with an environment to achieve it, potentially returning to the\nhuman for further information or judgement. \nDuring execution, it's crucial for the agents to gain “ground truth” from the environment at each step\n(such as tool call results or code execution) to assess its progress. Agents can then pause for human\nfeedback at checkpoints or when encountering blockers. The task often terminates upon completion, but\nit’s also common to include stopping conditions (such as a maximum number of iterations) to maintain\ncontrol.\n\nTheir implementation is often straightforward. They are typically just LLMs using tools based on\nenvironmental feedback in a loop. \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nAgents can be used for open-ended problems where it’s difficult or impossible to predict the required\nnumber of steps, and where you can’t hardcode a fixed path. ",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "An LLM agent runs tools in a loop to achieve a goal.\n\nAgents begin their work with either a command from, or interactive discussion with, the human user. Once the goal is set, agents plan and interact with an environment to achieve it, potentially returning to the human for further information or judgement. \nDuring execution, it's crucial for the agents to gain “ground truth” from the environment at each step (such as tool call results or code execution) to assess its progress. Agents can then pause for human feedback at checkpoints or when encountering blockers. The task often terminates upon completion, but it’s also common to include stopping conditions (such as a maximum number of iterations) to maintain control.\n\nTheir implementation is often straightforward. They are typically just LLMs using tools based on environmental feedback in a loop. \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nAgents can be used for open-ended problems where it’s difficult or impossible to predict the required number of steps, and where you can’t hardcode a fixed path. ",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": 1791802801645,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
