      "text": "Tool/Function Calling",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 7,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Tool/Function Calling",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.15,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "Kqqem5MRLrsupxiewIWiN",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -4693.797939235457,
      "y": 5619.810690281283,
      "width": 1026.778076171875,
      "height": 2450,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "TIlGYcnF4sKhSO55EycSy",
        "XamkCiS0zDBoC8Vst-QEf",
        "-NtU0l6WWns9tFkcqJIfd",
        "sFZK-tOS_RDRgxSkDZxAh",
        "ruVhogUhBzZ8Czyv4aMTd",
        "z8PnsKXFB9FZaNWIa_hLl",
        "kcoTZw6OVWUJUmcz3tM2J",
        "_4z9vxS07kIYQOTbArQDv",
        "mkSL4Kup1ejBnNFptaVmk",
        "05DeJlBHvA6njYbHaFgQe",
        "K50di04ipW3CxIOajtam0"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b8n",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1887842879,
      "version": 9380,
      "versionNonce": 396958129,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1792409321299,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "When we traditionally write software, we’re establishing a contract between deterministic systems. For\ninstance, a function call like getWeather(“NYC”) will always fetch the weather in New York City in the\nexact same manner every time it is called.\n\nTools are a new kind of software which reflects a contract between deterministic systems and non-\ndeterministic agents.\n\nA function call or tool call refers to a special kind of response we can get from the model if it examines\na prompt, and then determines that in order to follow the instructions in the prompt, it needs to call\none of the tools we made available to it.\n\nIf the model receives a prompt like “what is the weather in Paris?” in an API request, it could respond\nto that prompt with a tool call for the get_weather tool, with Paris as the location argument. \n\nA function call output or tool call output refers to the response a tool generates using the input from\na model’s tool call. The tool call output can either be structured JSON or plain text, and it should\ncontain a reference to a specific model tool call\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nù\n\n\n\n\n\n                curl -s http://localhost:11434/api/chat -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" -d '{\n                  \"model\": \"qwen3\",\n                  \"messages\": [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"What is the temperature in New York?\"}],\n                  \"stream\": false,\n                  \"tools\": [\n                    {\n                      \"type\": \"function\",\n                      \"function\": {\n                        \"name\": \"get_temperature\",\n                        \"description\": \"Get the current temperature for a city\",\n                        \"parameters\": {\n                          \"type\": \"object\",\n                          \"required\": [\"city\"],\n                          \"properties\": {\n                            \"city\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"description\": \"The name of the city\"}\n                          }\n                        }\n                      }\n                    }\n                  ]\n                }'\n\n\nPrinciples for writing effective tools\n\nChoosing the right tools for agents. More tools don’t always lead to better outcomes. building a few\nthoughtful tools targeting specific high-impact workflows\n    - Instead of implementing a read_logs tool, consider implementing a search_logs tool which only\nreturns relevant log lines and some surrounding context.\n    - Instead of implementing get_customer_by_id, list_transactions, and list_notes tools, implement a\nget_customer_context tool which compiles all of a customer’s recent & relevant information all at once.\n\nNamespacing your tools. AI agents will potentially gain access to dozens of MCP servers and hundreds\nof different tools–including those by other developers. Namespacing (grouping related tools under\ncommon prefixes) can help delineate boundaries between lots of tools (e.g., asana_search, jira_search)\nand by resource (e.g., asana_projects_search, asana_users_search), can help agents select the right\ntools at the right time.\n\nReturning meaningful context from your tools. tool implementations should take care to return only high\nsignal information back to agents. They should prioritize contextual relevance over flexibility, and\neschew low-level technical identifiers (for example: uuid, 256px_image_url, mime_type)\n\nOptimizing tool responses for token efficiency. Optimizing the quality of context is important. But so is\noptimizing the quantity of context returned back to agents in tool responses. For Claude Code, we\nrestrict tool responses to 25,000 tokens by default.\n\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "When we traditionally write software, we’re establishing a contract between deterministic systems. For instance, a function call like getWeather(“NYC”) will always fetch the weather in New York City in the exact same manner every time it is called.\n\nTools are a new kind of software which reflects a contract between deterministic systems and non-deterministic agents.\n\nA function call or tool call refers to a special kind of response we can get from the model if it examines a prompt, and then determines that in order to follow the instructions in the prompt, it needs to call one of the tools we made available to it.\n\nIf the model receives a prompt like “what is the weather in Paris?” in an API request, it could respond to that prompt with a tool call for the get_weather tool, with Paris as the location argument. \n\nA function call output or tool call output refers to the response a tool generates using the input from a model’s tool call. The tool call output can either be structured JSON or plain text, and it should contain a reference to a specific model tool call\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nù\n\n\n\n\n\n                curl -s http://localhost:11434/api/chat -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" -d '{\n                  \"model\": \"qwen3\",\n                  \"messages\": [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"What is the temperature in New York?\"}],\n                  \"stream\": false,\n                  \"tools\": [\n                    {\n                      \"type\": \"function\",\n                      \"function\": {\n                        \"name\": \"get_temperature\",\n                        \"description\": \"Get the current temperature for a city\",\n                        \"parameters\": {\n                          \"type\": \"object\",\n                          \"required\": [\"city\"],\n                          \"properties\": {\n                            \"city\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"description\": \"The name of the city\"}\n                          }\n                        }\n                      }\n                    }\n                  ]\n                }'\n\n\nPrinciples for writing effective tools\n\nChoosing the right tools for agents. More tools don’t always lead to better outcomes. building a few thoughtful tools targeting specific high-impact workflows\n    - Instead of implementing a read_logs tool, consider implementing a search_logs tool which only returns relevant log lines and some surrounding context.\n    - Instead of implementing get_customer_by_id, list_transactions, and list_notes tools, implement a get_customer_context tool which compiles all of a customer’s recent & relevant information all at once.\n\nNamespacing your tools. AI agents will potentially gain access to dozens of MCP servers and hundreds of different tools–including those by other developers. Namespacing (grouping related tools under common prefixes) can help delineate boundaries between lots of tools (e.g., asana_search, jira_search) and by resource (e.g., asana_projects_search, asana_users_search), can help agents select the right tools at the right time.\n\nReturning meaningful context from your tools. tool implementations should take care to return only high signal information back to agents. They should prioritize contextual relevance over flexibility, and eschew low-level technical identifiers (for example: uuid, 256px_image_url, mime_type)\n\nOptimizing tool responses for token efficiency. Optimizing the quality of context is important. But so is optimizing the quantity of context returned back to agents in tool responses. For Claude Code, we restrict tool responses to 25,000 tokens by default.\n\n\n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": 1791844403243,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
