      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "ReAct (Reason+Act)",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 7,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "ReAct (Reason+Act)",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.15,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "N7e-UpjuWhUGmzbHOUDs7",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -7906.3637369785865,
      "y": 5830.326070952799,
      "width": 1026.778076171875,
      "height": 2250,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "VsB6969c5U_yQ-wnXSB0p",
        "QDtuaGdP0aJDnuKjreY-U",
        "I5jiCFjjrjrLeHfeE3zOl",
        "ZgH3lvUiIWRBEVBIToKI6",
        "lb05GEuH_evrvncXKpmRt",
        "ImHXou-40BeH9rAVwLi8d",
        "eyioQAIpKW9rL9T7gU4hk",
        "TpgGXn0ygMwj6oVKQF4Yi",
        "N58oZ879lLLB8mpj9ZAyk",
        "R3QIfvyVtxf0rk1vT8Oc3"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b8q",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1450952351,
      "version": 9137,
      "versionNonce": 1179337215,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1792409321299,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Agents do not generate they act!\n\nThe authors propose a remarkably simple but powerful idea: instead of treating reasoning and acting as\nseparate processes, we should combine them within a single, iterative workflow. They call this approach\nReAct, short for Reasoning and Acting.\n\nChain-of-Thought, instead of asking a model to immediately provide the final answer, we encourage it to\ngenerate intermediate reasoning steps.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThe model is reasoning primarily from information encoded in its parameters and from the context\navailable in the prompt. If it does not know a fact, or if it remembers that fact incorrectly, it may\ncontinue building a logical argument on top of an incorrect assumption.\n\nInstead of encouraging the model to reason, we provide it with a set of actions that it can execute.\nFor example, an agent may be able to search Wikipedia. This is what the paper calls an Act-only\napproach. However, an agent that simply maps observations to actions may struggle with complex tasks.\n\nThe key insight of the paper is that we should not have to choose between these two capabilities.\nInstead, reasoning should guide actions, and actions should provide information that improves reasoning.\n\nReAct organizes the agent's behavior into a sequence of reasoning steps, actions, and observations.\nThe model can generate a thought, decide on an action, receive feedback from the environment, and then\nuse that feedback to decide what to do next.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nA Thought is a reasoning step generated by the language model. It can represent a hypothesis, a plan, a\nsummary of available information, or a decision about which action should be performed next.\nAn Action is an operation that interacts with the environment. This might be searching a knowledge\nbase, navigating a webpage, or manipulating an object in a simulated environment.\nAn Observation is the feedback returned by the environment after an action is executed.\n\nThe authors evaluate the methods on two different tasks.\nHotpotQA evaluates multi-hop question answering, where answering a question requires connecting\nmultiple pieces of information, often from different Wikipedia articles.\nFEVER evaluates fact verification. Given a statement, the model must determine whether the available\nevidence supports it, refutes it, or is insufficient to reach a conclusion.\n\nGreatest advantage emerges when internal reasoning and external evidence retrieval are combined\nstrategically!",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Agents do not generate they act!\n\nThe authors propose a remarkably simple but powerful idea: instead of treating reasoning and acting as separate processes, we should combine them within a single, iterative workflow. They call this approach ReAct, short for Reasoning and Acting.\n\nChain-of-Thought, instead of asking a model to immediately provide the final answer, we encourage it to generate intermediate reasoning steps.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThe model is reasoning primarily from information encoded in its parameters and from the context available in the prompt. If it does not know a fact, or if it remembers that fact incorrectly, it may continue building a logical argument on top of an incorrect assumption.\n\nInstead of encouraging the model to reason, we provide it with a set of actions that it can execute. For example, an agent may be able to search Wikipedia. This is what the paper calls an Act-only approach. However, an agent that simply maps observations to actions may struggle with complex tasks.\n\nThe key insight of the paper is that we should not have to choose between these two capabilities.\nInstead, reasoning should guide actions, and actions should provide information that improves reasoning.\n\nReAct organizes the agent's behavior into a sequence of reasoning steps, actions, and observations.\nThe model can generate a thought, decide on an action, receive feedback from the environment, and then use that feedback to decide what to do next.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nA Thought is a reasoning step generated by the language model. It can represent a hypothesis, a plan, a summary of available information, or a decision about which action should be performed next.\nAn Action is an operation that interacts with the environment. This might be searching a knowledge base, navigating a webpage, or manipulating an object in a simulated environment.\nAn Observation is the feedback returned by the environment after an action is executed.\n\nThe authors evaluate the methods on two different tasks.\nHotpotQA evaluates multi-hop question answering, where answering a question requires connecting multiple pieces of information, often from different Wikipedia articles.\nFEVER evaluates fact verification. Given a statement, the model must determine whether the available evidence supports it, refutes it, or is insufficient to reach a conclusion.\n\nGreatest advantage emerges when internal reasoning and external evidence retrieval are combined strategically!",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": 1791871982046,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
    {
      "id": "E0fyavvk-ag5FeC8BnVau",
