      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 817896863,
      "version": 741,
      "versionNonce": 861846911,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1792409321299,
      "created": 1791804351370,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Security of MCP",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 7,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Security of MCP",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.15,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "hhsEz1yo8FzP0i6MGzMCz",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -1707.7998347490175,
      "y": 5605.756182779103,
      "width": 1026.778076171875,
      "height": 1600,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "YSgOQdzT3fcJILLkewo4d",
        "9AH6Ps0HS-6sftI7iv7C7",
        "osdTw3sZQJJC0Sz-ibLYW",
        "Ec8t3rr9jjSIFvt5G_tlj",
        "NHiAIKpTG506oEj3ikO0D",
        "iDSH0ONjC6eUMJ3J-MCuM",
        "_G0iHzD2mQ0sTVnQO9WfC",
        "lsaN_sqkF3ey1JWxqWHDc",
        "3Lnxmxe3GFMAzztCuGYFJ",
        "jI00N73inoDXqtZlr3o8x"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b9l",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2135495103,
      "version": 8959,
      "versionNonce": 1159011825,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1792409321299,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "15 security problems discussed in the NSA document Model Context Protocol (MCP): Security Design\nConsiderations for AI-Driven Automation\n\nWeak Access Control. MCP does not require authentication or role-based authorization. Many\nimplementations lack granular permissions for reading, creating, modifying, or deleting resources. This can\nallow agents and connected services to access sensitive information without appropriate restrictions.\n\nInsecure Context and Data Serialization. MCP exchanges structured messages containing data, context,\nand configuration information. Without strict schema enforcement and validation, these messages may\ncarry malicious instructions or executable content, potentially leading to injection attacks.\n\nPoor Approval Workflows. Some MCP implementations do not enforce explicit user approval for tool\noperations. Additionally, previously authorized servers may change their capabilities or access additional\nresources without renewed consent, allowing potentially dangerous actions to occur unnoticed.\n\nToken and Session Security Vulnerabilities. MCP implementations may rely on bearer tokens and session\nidentifiers without enforcing expiration, rotation, revocation, or replay protection. Attackers who obtain\nvalid credentials or session information could impersonate legitimate clients and perform unauthorized\noperations.\n\nMisconfigurations and Poor Isolation. MCP servers are often deployed using open-source frameworks\nwithout adequate security configuration. Insufficient separation between tools, tasks, and datasets\ncan expose sensitive information or allow compromised components to affect unrelated workflows.\n\nInconsistent and Unpredictable Agent Behavior. Different MCP implementations may interpret the same\nmessages or context differently. Because AI agents depend on probabilistic reasoning and previous\ninteractions, attackers may manipulate context to influence decisions and produce unexpected or unsafe\nbehavior.\n\nInsufficient Audit Logging. MCP does not mandate comprehensive security logging. Implementations may\nfail to record tool executions, authorization failures, message origins, or suspicious activities, making it\ndifficult to detect attacks, investigate incidents, and establish accountability.\n\nDenial of Service and Resource Exhaustion. Attackers can overwhelm MCP servers through malformed\ninputs, recursive task requests, or computationally expensive operations. These attacks can exhaust\nprocessing resources, slow down agent workflows, and disrupt connected services.\n\nTool Parameter Injection. MCP tools may execute parameters received from agents without sufficient\nvalidation. Maliciously crafted inputs can manipulate legitimate tool functionality, potentially exposing\nsensitive information or triggering unauthorized command execution.\n\nTool Invocation Path Confusion. MCP clients may resolve tool names dynamically from registries or local\nmodules. Attackers can exploit naming collisions or manipulated configurations to redirect tool\ninvocations toward malicious components, potentially hijacking execution workflows.\n\nExcessive Repository Permissions. Some MCP integrations, such as GitHub-connected tools, can receive\nbroad access to both private and public repositories. A compromised or malicious tool could retrieve\nconfidential source code and publish it externally without the user's explicit authorization.\n\nMalicious MCP Servers and Tool Poisoning. Attackers can introduce malicious MCP servers that\nmanipulate tool descriptions or advertised capabilities. The NSA describes a WhatsApp integration\nscenario in which a malicious server influenced the agent to expose private messages. The server initially\nappeared legitimate before changing its behavior.\n\nPrompt Injection Through Tool Outputs. MCP tools may return content containing hidden instructions\nthat an AI agent mistakenly treats as authoritative commands. These instructions can redirect agent\nbehavior, trigger unauthorized tool calls, or cause sensitive information to be disclosed.\n\nCross-Agent Output Poisoning. In multi-agent workflows, the output of one agent frequently becomes\nthe input of another. If malicious content is not validated, it can propagate across the automation\npipeline, causing cascading prompt injections, data exfiltration, and execution-flow manipulation.\n\nArbitrary and Remote Code Execution. Vulnerable MCP implementations may accept untrusted inputs that\nultimately trigger operating-system commands or other executable operations.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "15 security problems discussed in the NSA document Model Context Protocol (MCP): Security Design Considerations for AI-Driven Automation\n\nWeak Access Control. MCP does not require authentication or role-based authorization. Many implementations lack granular permissions for reading, creating, modifying, or deleting resources. This can allow agents and connected services to access sensitive information without appropriate restrictions.\n\nInsecure Context and Data Serialization. MCP exchanges structured messages containing data, context, and configuration information. Without strict schema enforcement and validation, these messages may carry malicious instructions or executable content, potentially leading to injection attacks.\n\nPoor Approval Workflows. Some MCP implementations do not enforce explicit user approval for tool operations. Additionally, previously authorized servers may change their capabilities or access additional resources without renewed consent, allowing potentially dangerous actions to occur unnoticed.\n\nToken and Session Security Vulnerabilities. MCP implementations may rely on bearer tokens and session identifiers without enforcing expiration, rotation, revocation, or replay protection. Attackers who obtain valid credentials or session information could impersonate legitimate clients and perform unauthorized operations.\n\nMisconfigurations and Poor Isolation. MCP servers are often deployed using open-source frameworks without adequate security configuration. Insufficient separation between tools, tasks, and datasets can expose sensitive information or allow compromised components to affect unrelated workflows.\n\nInconsistent and Unpredictable Agent Behavior. Different MCP implementations may interpret the same messages or context differently. Because AI agents depend on probabilistic reasoning and previous interactions, attackers may manipulate context to influence decisions and produce unexpected or unsafe behavior.\n\nInsufficient Audit Logging. MCP does not mandate comprehensive security logging. Implementations may fail to record tool executions, authorization failures, message origins, or suspicious activities, making it difficult to detect attacks, investigate incidents, and establish accountability.\n\nDenial of Service and Resource Exhaustion. Attackers can overwhelm MCP servers through malformed inputs, recursive task requests, or computationally expensive operations. These attacks can exhaust processing resources, slow down agent workflows, and disrupt connected services.\n\nTool Parameter Injection. MCP tools may execute parameters received from agents without sufficient validation. Maliciously crafted inputs can manipulate legitimate tool functionality, potentially exposing sensitive information or triggering unauthorized command execution.\n\nTool Invocation Path Confusion. MCP clients may resolve tool names dynamically from registries or local modules. Attackers can exploit naming collisions or manipulated configurations to redirect tool invocations toward malicious components, potentially hijacking execution workflows.\n\nExcessive Repository Permissions. Some MCP integrations, such as GitHub-connected tools, can receive broad access to both private and public repositories. A compromised or malicious tool could retrieve confidential source code and publish it externally without the user's explicit authorization.\n\nMalicious MCP Servers and Tool Poisoning. Attackers can introduce malicious MCP servers that manipulate tool descriptions or advertised capabilities. The NSA describes a WhatsApp integration scenario in which a malicious server influenced the agent to expose private messages. The server initially appeared legitimate before changing its behavior.\n\nPrompt Injection Through Tool Outputs. MCP tools may return content containing hidden instructions that an AI agent mistakenly treats as authoritative commands. These instructions can redirect agent behavior, trigger unauthorized tool calls, or cause sensitive information to be disclosed.\n\nCross-Agent Output Poisoning. In multi-agent workflows, the output of one agent frequently becomes the input of another. If malicious content is not validated, it can propagate across the automation pipeline, causing cascading prompt injections, data exfiltration, and execution-flow manipulation.\n\nArbitrary and Remote Code Execution. Vulnerable MCP implementations may accept untrusted inputs that ultimately trigger operating-system commands or other executable operations.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": 1791879603718,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
