      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "bxic7ZMbx9P3IaQ-BMESt",
        "Al2-47MRmN1G0Y18GlyBD",
        "VRCEYbhBQmCsTOaMeZos3",
        "D8L7f_gQ7yopERD9TcPDX",
        "4lRS7jHyfeQRl9F7NaJXn",
        "HG3Mrho3cijg2cK0zmZRC",
        "KsYm_4eXXhwAUr-vv_An5",
        "hm4Tz712g5Mjm_uGWWpLS",
        "v_Qoz2goyhEMeRG9TeG02"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bAQ",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1283536607,
      "version": 8832,
      "versionNonce": 1512951839,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1792409321299,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "System 1 and System 2 thinking describes two distinct modes of cognitive processing introduced by\nDaniel Kahneman in his book Thinking, Fast and Slow.\n\nSystem 1 Thinking: Our brains’ fast, automatic, unconscious, and emotional response to situations and\nstimuli. This can be in the form of absentmindedly reading text on a billboard, knowing how to tie your\nshoelaces without a second thought, or instinctively hopping over a puddle on the sidewalk.\n\nSystem 2 Thinking: The slow, effortful, and logical mode in which our brains operate when solving more\ncomplicated problems. For example, System 2 thinking is used when looking for a friend in a crowd,\nparking your vehicle in a tight space, or determining the quality-to-value ratio of your take-out lunch.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "System 1 and System 2 thinking describes two distinct modes of cognitive processing introduced by Daniel Kahneman in his book Thinking, Fast and Slow.\n\nSystem 1 Thinking: Our brains’ fast, automatic, unconscious, and emotional response to situations and stimuli. This can be in the form of absentmindedly reading text on a billboard, knowing how to tie your shoelaces without a second thought, or instinctively hopping over a puddle on the sidewalk.\n\nSystem 2 Thinking: The slow, effortful, and logical mode in which our brains operate when solving more complicated problems. For example, System 2 thinking is used when looking for a friend in a crowd, parking your vehicle in a tight space, or determining the quality-to-value ratio of your take-out lunch.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": 1791873643455,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
