      "version": 180,
      "versionNonce": 1615649983,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1792409321299,
      "created": 1791874487844,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Primitives (Questions)\n",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 7,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Primitives (Questions)\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.15,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "-MiN5U62kajxZs7l1Ybhg",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 3480.6402728602843,
      "y": 5384.302985482773,
      "width": 1026.778076171875,
      "height": 725,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [
        "Im4NwlrBSp3JYQpt1DR0G",
        "Fb1LidO_jFXESJA6_fgS-",
        "CfgkpF4kNe5jeFG4Tpelq",
        "FHVqhRLITOv50Mgfc2_Pk",
        "h714yPII0loEUCIXjWXHo",
        "7fhhA9LsBK8ny4jYCL58h",
        "jGBo9dquXSlWUV2xmrJUH",
        "eCVUHZPkNVenYbeNUGRUO",
        "lbKx0ShL6cYSjtG1vy-78",
        "OUXmo7AfxHgtep1Nfcdz4"
      ],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bAb",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 849289279,
      "version": 8977,
      "versionNonce": 1024168625,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1792409321299,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "System One models are a class of AI models built to make fast, structured decisions that software can\nuse directly. A System One model evaluates a state and returns typed answers and probabilities.\n\nLike an LLM, a System One model understands natural-language input. It returns typed decisions and\nprobabilities rather than generated text.\n\nprimitives are the small, typed building blocks you compose in code. They come in pairs: a question\ndefines one judgment for a System One model to make about a state, and its answer is the typed value\nthat comes back.\n\nThere are three question types, each returning a different shape of answer.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n- Choice fits when the answer is one of a known set of options with no order between them: routing a\nticket to a department, classifying a document type, detecting a programming language. Give the full list\nof options, and add an other or none of the above option when the list might not cover every input.\n- Score fits when the answer falls on a spectrum and you can describe what each point on that\nspectrum means: bug severity, customer frustration, skill level. The levels are yours to define, and the\nmodel returns a position along them.\n- Noul fits a clean yes/no question where the probability itself is the useful signal: does this message\ncontain personally identifiable information, is the customer requesting a refund, does the resume mention\ndistributed systems.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "System One models are a class of AI models built to make fast, structured decisions that software can use directly. A System One model evaluates a state and returns typed answers and probabilities.\n\nLike an LLM, a System One model understands natural-language input. It returns typed decisions and probabilities rather than generated text.\n\nprimitives are the small, typed building blocks you compose in code. They come in pairs: a question defines one judgment for a System One model to make about a state, and its answer is the typed value that comes back.\n\nThere are three question types, each returning a different shape of answer.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n- Choice fits when the answer is one of a known set of options with no order between them: routing a ticket to a department, classifying a document type, detecting a programming language. Give the full list of options, and add an other or none of the above option when the list might not cover every input.\n- Score fits when the answer falls on a spectrum and you can describe what each point on that spectrum means: bug severity, customer frustration, skill level. The levels are yours to define, and the model returns a position along them.\n- Noul fits a clean yes/no question where the probability itself is the useful signal: does this message contain personally identifiable information, is the customer requesting a refund, does the resume mention distributed systems.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "created": 1791874549841,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
